  "assumptions": [
    {
      "what": "the 32nd row",
      "reason": "The Mini's pool carries `0050` twice, adjacent (#15 and #16). The owner's reading has it once, at bar 6. Written as read. If the iC shares the full 32, the second belongs in bar 6 as well and the only effect is how often that bar throws `0050`."
    },
    {
      "what": "which row of a bar is thrown",
      "reason": "A bar holds up to four rows and nothing read says how one is chosen. The Mini picks per count through stage and attribute; the iC may do the same, or roll. Random until a device says otherwise."
    },
    {
      "what": "rhythm timing windows",
      "reason": "The September 2026 recordings disprove a static RPM-to-bar ramp. RPMRhythm uses recent press spacing, a progressively narrower pace window, and a stall before repeated mistakes lose bars. These windows are approximations, not recovered firmware. See utilities/claude/docs/design/ic_rpm.md. The old claim that screen bars disagree with transmitted attacks was a packet decoding error: the pattern is in packet 4, and the corrected captures agree with the bar table."
    },
    {
      "what": "the interval-to-RPM conversion",
      "reason": "Both the device and Omnipet use button presses. The footage shows a recent-speed readout in steps of five, capped at 99. The interval calibration in rpm_rhythm.py is an approximation from visible movement, not a literal revolutions-per-minute conversion or a measurement of electrical button contacts."
    }
  ]
